<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">KGB</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Committee for State Security</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The KGB (Committee for State Security) was the main security agency of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from 1954 until 1991, responsible for foreign intelligence, counter-intelligence, border troops and the suppression of political dissent. It succeeded the security services descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> and kept close watch on Soviet society and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a>. Its chairman Vladimir Kryuchkov took part in the failed coup of August 1991, after which it was dissolved.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kgb.jpg" alt="KGB" width="320" height="617" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jgaray · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblema_KGB.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The KGB was formed in March 1954 under Ivan Serov, after a restructuring of the MVD that followed the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavrentiy-beria">Lavrentiy Beria</a> in June 1953. It was the direct successor of earlier Soviet secret police agencies, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogpu">OGPU</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a>. Attached to the Council of Ministers, it held what was called union-republican jurisdiction: similar agencies operated in each republic apart from the Russian SFSR, where the KGB itself was headquartered. It was a military service governed by army laws and regulations, and its operational personnel held army-style ranks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Within the Soviet bloc the KGB monitored public and private opinion and possible plots. It was instrumental in crushing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-revolution-of-1956">Hungarian Revolution of 1956</a>, when Serov personally supervised the &quot;normalization&quot; of the country after the invasion. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>'s invasion that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-spring">Prague Spring</a> in 1968, it infiltrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> with illegal residents disguised as Western tourists, who were to spy on supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-dubcek">Alexander Dubček</a>'s government and plant evidence that right-wing groups backed by Western agencies planned a coup.</p><p>It also prepared hardline members of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, such as Alois Indra and Vasiľ Škultéty, to take power. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in the 1980s it infiltrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and coordinated the declaration of martial law with Wojciech Jaruzelski, but Solidarity weakened the Polish government by 1989. On 27 December 1979 KGB special forces, the Alpha and Zenith groups, joined by a GRU battalion and paratroopers, stormed the Tajbeg Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafizullah-amin">Hafizullah Amin</a>.</p><p>Under Vladimir Kryuchkov, chairman from 1988, the KGB led the coup attempt of August 1991 against President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>. Its failure and the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> led to the dissolution of the KGB on 3 December 1991. Its successors in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> were the Foreign Intelligence Service (SVR) and what became the Federal Security Service (FSB).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The KGB had two main parts, the organs and the troops. The organs handled intelligence, counter-intelligence and military counter-intelligence. The troops, separate from the armed forces, included the Border Troops, the Governmental Signals Troops and the Kremlin Regiment, with the special forces Alpha and Vympel. The First Chief Directorate ran foreign espionage, and its Directorate S ran illegals, agents working under deep cover abroad. The Second Chief Directorate handled counter-intelligence and internal political control. The Fifth, which dealt with artistic, political and religious dissent, was renamed Directorate Z in 1989. Abroad, legal residents worked from embassies under diplomatic immunity, while illegals had no such protection.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “KGB” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
